inimizing edge effects the  easiest way to look at that is making  sure that your plates are lidded during  their incubation times I would keep  incubation times themselves as short as  possible while ensuring maximum  performance within your assay other  things that generally escape notice are  your lab environment in general  high degree of air-conditioning can  cause very dry air which can increase  evaporation looking at the assay volume  versus the plate type that you're  working with for example if you're using  a 96-well plate and doing a 50 micro  litre eliza a small degree of  evaporation is going to have a much  larger effect and say if you were doing  100 micro liter eliza or doing it within  three four well plate a much smaller  available surface area for that  evaporation to take place the another  way of looking at it is incubating your  plates with any humidified environment  to keep that evaporation to a minimum  a lot of it depends on the resources  that you have available in the in  general environment that you're working  in  and of our next question is two parts  now based on your experience how easy  was it to program an Eliza on the  biomech and what advice recommendations  you have for an automation novice in my  personal experience is actually quite  easy to program an Eliza protocol  onto a biomech assuming that you're  working with a biomech as a standalone  liquid handler it's basically just a  series of single reagent transfer steps  um and those can be done basically the  same protocol with just a different  source reagent and that's probably one  of the easiest methods to set up within  the biomech software that gets obviously  a little bit more complicated if you  start integrating components on back of  a within a biomech or a biomech or any  other liquid handler is part of an  integrated system  setting up liquid handling protocols  around some of these assays the best  advice I can give for especially for  novice users is don't overthink it if  thing you need to do is add a reagent to  a plate as a it's a simple one single  reagent transfer  making a method overly complex is not  going to benefit you in the long run  that obviously changes once you get into  scheduled environments the level of  complexity increases when you're talking  about integrated devices in the  scheduled environment  we'll say within Sammie as an example of  a scheduled environment setting up an  Eliza protocol is also quite simple the  the most part comes when optimizing that  method for an optimal timing it which is  a part of what I touched on towards the  end of the presentation there  Thank You Philip and a reminder to our  audience um if you'd like to submit any  questions but there is a drop-down box  located on the far left of your  presentation window labeled ask a  question and just type in your question  and click send  okay our next question Philip what's  next do you take to minimize sample to  sample contamination during automation  well there's multiple ways that you can  look at this and it also depends on what  types of samples that you're referring  to some people in will say an effort to  reduce consumables costs Institute tip  wash and most liquid handling platforms  biomech included usually come with the  available option of a tip wash station I  would recommend  a very hard evaluation very stringent  evaluation of your tip wash protocol  also I'm using new tips for every sample  an easy way to avoid cross contamination  using barrier tips is another way if  you're working in more of a molecular  biology standpoint or a phage standpoint  barrier tips would be a necessity I work  a lot with cell culture supernatants and  hybridoma cultures so we have hepa  enclosures around several or apart  liquid handlers to reduce broad  contamination overall like I said  there's it depends a lot on the types of  samples that you're working with in the  types of contamination or cross  contamination that you're worried about  but there's usually multiple ways to  approach those problems  Thank You Philip here's another question  how do you go about troubleshooting and  automated fa  oh very good question this is a primary  reason why benchtop to automation  transfer is so vital  it's all up your ass a method at the  bench manually and you know that your  assay is performing as expected moving  that over to a liquid handling platform  or an integrated platform you start to  see discrepancies or drift it's not the  chemistry or the biology of the assay  that's your problem  way that  think to go about something like that is  and it does take a bit of a familiarity  with the components that you're working  with looking at your point and  identifying the possible causes and then  changing one variable at a time across  your process to identify what part of  that automation process is your culprit  so to speak it does have to be a very  methodical approach and that is a big  reason why I never recommend developing  a G's on automation platforms I would  highly recommend developing them  manually at the bench and then  transferring to an automated platform it  takes a whole set of variables off the  table if you do start to see problems  Philip and it looks like we have time  for one more question but I do want to  remind our audience I've any questions  we did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  type of registration okay Philip our  last question how do you access and  visualize data from your automated  assets well there's a couple of things  that come into consideration with that  personally the softwares that we use  here at bi in in my lab here are  primarily graphpad prism and Spotfire  for doing data visualizations accessing  them is where you can run into some  problems sometimes network connectivity  is a big issue and it tends to be a  little easier in academia versus  corporate environments for example our  two automated platforms that we have in  my lab now one that was integrated by  PerkinElmer and one that was integrated  by a Beckman Coulter  neither of them are actually compatible  with our corporate firewall so those  platforms actually are not on our  network so we have to physically  transfer those files through a gateway  computer those restrictions tend to be  tend to vary I guess we'll say from  company to company and academic  institution academic institution but the  as far as the actual data processing and  data visualization the ones that we use  our prism in Spotfire and I'm sure that  there's going to be a preference with  that with most users  thank you again Philip Corman and thank  you to our sponsor Beckman Coulter  Lifesciences for underwriting today's  educational webcast  webcasts can't be viewed on tube and  through August 2019 labyrinths will  alert you via email when it's available  for replay we encourage you to share  that email with your colleagues who may  have missed today's live event until  next time goodbye

